From 18866e1bc02648c978251da47afc4d06dcdc21a1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Javier Maestro <jjmaestro@ieee.org>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 05:44:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] patch: let the PostgreSQL version probe be preset

configure establishes which PostgreSQL it is building against by running
`$PG_CONFIG --version` and parsing the output. Executing `pg_config` is fine
natively and impossible when cross-compiling: `$PG_CONFIG` is then a
target-architecture binary the build host cannot run, so the probe yields
nothing, the version parse finds no match, and configure stops with "Citus is
not compatible with the detected PostgreSQL version".

The version is not something only that binary knows, so accept it from the
environment and fall back to the probe when it is unset:

    citusac_pg_config_version=${citusac_pg_config_version:-$($PG_CONFIG --version 2>/dev/null)}

A cross-compiling caller exports `citusac_pg_config_version="PostgreSQL
$PG_VERSION_NUM"` and everything downstream, the compatibility check
included, proceeds unchanged. A native build sets nothing and still probes.

Both `configure` and `configure.ac` carry the change, so regenerating
configure keeps it.
---
 configure    | 8 +++++++-
 configure.ac | 8 +++++++-
 2 files changed, 14 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/configure b/configure
index e98d8d968..48874be7f 100755
--- a/configure
+++ b/configure
@@ -2548,7 +2548,13 @@ if test -z "$PG_CONFIG"; then
 fi
 
 # check we're building against a supported version of PostgreSQL
-citusac_pg_config_version=$($PG_CONFIG --version 2>/dev/null)
+#
+# `$PG_CONFIG --version` is a runtime probe; when cross-compiling, `$PG_CONFIG`
+# is a target-arch binary that cannot execute on the build host. A preset
+# `citusac_pg_config_version` env var bypasses the probe and is consumed by the
+# version extraction below as if `pg_config` had emitted it. Cross-compiling
+# callers pass `citusac_pg_config_version="PostgreSQL $PG_VERSION_NUM"`.
+citusac_pg_config_version=${citusac_pg_config_version:-$($PG_CONFIG --version 2>/dev/null)}
 # Extract major[.minor] from "PostgreSQL X[.Y] ..."
 version_num=$(printf '%s\n' "$citusac_pg_config_version" \
   | $SED -nE 's/^PostgreSQL[[:space:]]+([0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?).*/\1/p')
diff --git a/configure.ac b/configure.ac
index 0a46bc4a4..0e0b9f386 100644
--- a/configure.ac
+++ b/configure.ac
@@ -63,7 +63,13 @@ if test -z "$PG_CONFIG"; then
 fi
 
 # check we're building against a supported version of PostgreSQL
-citusac_pg_config_version=$($PG_CONFIG --version 2>/dev/null)
+#
+# `$PG_CONFIG --version` is a runtime probe; when cross-compiling, `$PG_CONFIG`
+# is a target-arch binary that cannot execute on the build host. A preset
+# `citusac_pg_config_version` env var bypasses the probe and is consumed by the
+# version extraction below as if `pg_config` had emitted it. Cross-compiling
+# callers pass `citusac_pg_config_version="PostgreSQL $PG_VERSION_NUM"`.
+citusac_pg_config_version=${citusac_pg_config_version:-$($PG_CONFIG --version 2>/dev/null)}
 version_num=$(echo "$citusac_pg_config_version"|
               $SED -e 's/^PostgreSQL \([[0-9]]*\)\(\.[[0-9]]*\)\{0,1\}\(.*\)$/\1\2/')
 
